1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,800
EpitopeRank, "Splice-derived neoantigen candidates":
from a single splicing event to peptides worth testing at the bench.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,600
Project test2 already holds 100,512 splicing events.
The platform checks the data first: the coding-sequence list is missing,
and that list is the precondition for this workflow.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:14,400
Step two: enter the subject's HLA class I types,
HLA-A*02:01 and HLA-B*07:02, then click "Generate candidate peptides".

4
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:19,600
Three steps light up in order:
load reference coding sequences, parse translatable events, predict HLA binding.

5
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:25,200
Only peptides that span the splice junction and translate into 8-11 aa
enter the candidate pool; peptides already present in the normal proteome are removed.

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:30,600
That is also why it never runs end-to-end unattended:
every stage is a human confirmation gate, and every skip or failure is logged.

7
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:36,400
At 81% the last step begins: HLA binding prediction via IEDB NetMHCpan 4.1 EL,
scoring affinity and Rank% per peptide against the real HLA types.

8
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:42,400
Done: 132 candidate peptides derived from 486 translatable splicing events,
saved into the project directory.

9
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:48,000
The candidate table lists sequence, length, source event, HLA type,
predicted affinity (nM) and Rank%, with filtering and CSV export.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Row one, ALVPGTSSK: 9 residues, from event SE_0123,
HLA-A*02:01, predicted affinity 12.4 nM, Rank 0.12%.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,800
Mind the boundary: binding prediction is not presentation, and not immunogenicity.
This is a shortlist worth validating experimentally, not a conclusion.
